=== task: multi medium
title: One pass is enough
prompt: Each of these answers a question about a list of n items. Choose **every** one that is O(n).
* Removing duplicates with a `seen` set and a result list :: Right. One pass with O(1) checks.
* Finding whether two numbers add up to a target, with a set of the numbers seen so far :: Right. One pass, one O(1) lookup per number.
* Finding the first repeat by checking each item against a `seen` set :: Right. One pass.
- Checking every pair of items to see whether any two are equal :: That's n(n − 1)/2 comparisons: O(n²).
- Calling `items.count(x)` for every item x to find a repeat :: Each `count` walks the whole list: O(n²).
- Sorting the list first and then comparing neighbours :: The sort alone is O(n log n).
hint: Which ones make a single pass with O(1) work per item?
hint: `count` and checking pairs both hide a second loop.
right: The seen-before pattern turns "have I met this before?" into an O(1) set check, so one pass is enough.
wrong: O(n): removing duplicates, two-sum and first repeat with a set. Not O(n): every pair (O(n²)), `count` for every item (O(n²)), and sorting first (O(n log n)).

